[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Creating a node in memory costs 5 ms, and every meta field matches a rebuild

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-SCRIBE-NEW-IN-ONE-LOAD
TITLE: Creating a node in memory costs 5 ms, and every meta field matches a rebuild
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: strictdoc-trial worktree, 382-node canon copied to a temp dir, warm cache; strictdoc 0.28.3; 2026-09-01
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured in the strictdoc-trial worktree against the 382-node canon copied to
a temporary directory, warm cache, through the same harness before and after,
each run confirmed to write the files it claimed before its timing was read:

    three creates, skeleton-to-disk then reload    2327 ms
    three creates, built in memory                   16 ms
    three sets (unchanged, the comparison)           18 ms
    the one full reload the batch defers            630 ms

So a create went from about 776 ms to about 5 ms, and a run of them now pays
one reload rather than one each.

WHAT MADE IT AVAILABLE: the corpus holds 382 documents and 382 DocumentGrammar
objects around exactly ONE shared element list. The reload the create used to
pay re-read 382 files to obtain a reference to an object already in memory.

WHAT MADE IT SAFE: the meta is the one thing derived by hand, so it is
compared rather than trusted. A document built in memory was compared against
one from a genuine rebuild, field for field: 11 of 11 DocumentMeta fields
identical, at four depths -- a plan directory, docs/spec, the repository root
where the empty-directory branch is taken, and a hidden .sdoc/ directory
beside a package. The rendered bytes match as well.

THREE POSITIVE CONTROLS, each run by breaking the thing and watching the
harness say so:

* level derived one too deep -> the comparison fails naming `level`, 5 vs 4;
* the element list copied rather than shared -> the identity check fails;
* the unreadable-destination guard removed -> a create under output/ lands on
  disk and has_node is False after the next reload. That is the silent loss
  the guard exists for, and it was previously caught by accident, because the
  old shape looked the new document up in a reloaded tree and did not find it.
<<<
